1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:02,000
<u>Reliability & Failure Handling</u>

2
00:00:03,353 --> 00:00:04,503
<i>What is a partial failure?</i>

3
00:00:05,770 --> 00:00:10,850
A partial failure means one dependency, request, region, or component fails while the rest of the system continues.

4
00:00:11,550 --> 00:00:14,420
Distributed systems must define behavior for these mixed states.

5
00:00:15,921 --> 00:00:16,931
<i>What does a timeout mean?</i>

6
00:00:18,454 --> 00:00:20,234
A timeout means the caller stopped waiting.

7
00:00:20,944 --> 00:00:25,214
It does not prove that the remote operation failed; the server may have completed it after the caller gave up.

8
00:00:26,737 --> 00:00:28,157
<i>Why can retries be dangerous?</i>

9
00:00:29,678 --> 00:00:31,978
Retries multiply load and may repeat side effects.

10
00:00:32,668 --> 00:00:37,088
If the first request succeeded but its response was lost, a blind retry can apply the operation twice.

11
00:00:38,609 --> 00:00:40,029
<i>What is exponential backoff?</i>

12
00:00:41,550 --> 00:00:44,470
Exponential backoff increases the delay between retry attempts.

13
00:00:45,180 --> 00:00:48,740
It reduces pressure on an unhealthy dependency compared with rapid fixed retries.

14
00:00:50,265 --> 00:00:50,965
<i>What is jitter?</i>

15
00:00:52,486 --> 00:00:56,176
Jitter adds randomness to retry delays so many clients do not retry at the same instant.

16
00:00:56,886 --> 00:00:58,916
This helps avoid synchronized retry storms.

17
00:01:00,438 --> 00:01:01,608
<i>What is a retry budget?</i>

18
00:01:03,139 --> 00:01:06,759
A retry budget limits how much additional retry traffic a system is willing to create.

19
00:01:07,459 --> 00:01:10,419
It prevents recovery logic from becoming a second source of overload.

20
00:01:11,950 --> 00:01:13,200
<i>What is retry ownership?</i>

21
00:01:14,718 --> 00:01:18,638
Retry ownership defines which layer is responsible for retrying one logical operation.

22
00:01:19,348 --> 00:01:22,958
UI callbacks, HTTP clients, repositories, gateways, services,

23
00:01:23,128 --> 00:01:25,978
and background workers should not independently multiply the same retry.

24
00:01:27,849 --> 00:01:28,889
<i>What is idempotency?</i>

25
00:01:30,146 --> 00:01:34,316
An idempotent operation can be repeated without applying the same business effect multiple times.

26
00:01:34,996 --> 00:01:38,726
Idempotency is especially important when the outcome of a previous request is uncertain.

27
00:01:40,254 --> 00:01:41,574
<i>What is an idempotency key?</i>

28
00:01:43,099 --> 00:01:46,889
An idempotency key identifies one logical mutation across repeated requests.

29
00:01:47,589 --> 00:01:50,899
The service must persist the key and result within a defined scope and retention period;

30
00:01:51,109 --> 00:01:52,959
the header alone does not create idempotency.

31
00:01:54,478 --> 00:01:55,548
<i>What is a circuit breaker?</i>

32
00:01:57,083 --> 00:02:01,483
A circuit breaker temporarily stops calls to a failing dependency after a threshold, waits for a cooldown,

33
00:02:01,703 --> 00:02:04,113
and then allows limited probes before normal traffic resumes.

34
00:02:05,625 --> 00:02:06,915
<i>What is graceful degradation?</i>

35
00:02:08,758 --> 00:02:12,288
Graceful degradation keeps core behavior available when an optional dependency fails.

36
00:02:12,998 --> 00:02:17,908
A fallback must remain safe: stale recommendations may be acceptable, stale authorization decisions may not be.

37
00:02:19,186 --> 00:02:20,476
<i>What are liveness and readiness?</i>

38
00:02:21,979 --> 00:02:25,069
Liveness asks whether a process is alive enough that restarting it may help.

39
00:02:25,779 --> 00:02:28,599
Readiness asks whether an instance should currently receive traffic.

40
00:02:29,299 --> 00:02:34,049
Dependency health can be observed separately so one shared outage does not automatically remove every upstream instance.

41
00:02:35,556 --> 00:02:36,606
<i>What is observability?</i>

42
00:02:38,118 --> 00:02:42,768
Observability uses logs, metrics, traces, and domain signals to explain what happened in production.

43
00:02:43,478 --> 00:02:48,028
Useful signals include latency percentiles, error rate, saturation, queue age, dependency health,

44
00:02:48,148 --> 00:02:49,288
and user-visible outcomes.
